To make BGP sessions more resilient to brief link flips and prevent immediate session teardowns, adjust the BGP timers to extend the tolerance window for missed keepalives. Use the following configuration commands:

```cli
configure router bgp
    group "your_group_name"  # Replace with your BGP group name
        hold-time 300 min 300  # Increase hold timer to 300 seconds (5 minutes)
        keepalive 100          # Set keepalive interval to 100 seconds
    exit
exit
```

### Key Changes:
1. **Extended Hold Time**:  
   `hold-time 300 min 300` increases the maximum time BGP waits for a keepalive/update before declaring the neighbor down (from default 90 seconds to 300 seconds). The `min 300` ensures peers must support this higher value.

2. **Reduced Keepalive Frequency**:  
   `keepalive 100` sends keepalives every 100 seconds (instead of the default 30 seconds), reducing unnecessary traffic while aligning with the extended hold timer (`hold-time` should be ≥3x `keepalive`).

### Why This Works:
- BGP won't tear down the session immediately during a link "blip." The session only drops if the disruption lasts longer than the hold timer (300 seconds here).
- The configuration tolerates intermittent outages (e.g., fiber flaps) by giving the link time to recover before resetting the peering.

> **Note**:  
> - Replace `"your_group_name"` with your actual BGP group name. Apply to specific neighbors if needed.  
> - Coordinate with the peer to ensure compatible timer values to avoid session negotiation failures.  
> - Test during maintenance; excessively long timers may delay fault detection.
